---
/**
 * Seconds to minutes converter — 영국판 여섯 번째 도구(초·분·시간 변환). 한국어판 /tools/sec-convert/ 와
 * 계산(lib/sec-convert.ts)·스크립트(scripts/sec-convert.ts 의 EN 사전, 영어 판 공용)를 같이 쓰고, 문장은 영국 영어로 새로 썼다(번역 아님).
 * 조사표 영국 칸 '같음'(#91, SI 브로슈어 표 8) · #88 '시간 → 분/초'(#parts)도 같은 페이지.
 *
 * 소제목은 영국 구글 자동완성에서 왔다(keywords/gb-sec-convert-2026-10-21.csv, hl en · gl gb):
 *   seconds to minutes formula·chart·timer / decimal hours chart·for payroll·worked calculator·to hours and minutes /
 *   convert seconds to (days) hours minutes seconds formula·excel·google sheets·python·js·sql·bash /
 *   milliseconds to seconds to minutes to hours·formula·timer
 * 단위 값은 SI 브로슈어 표 8(1 min = 60 s, 1 h = 3600 s, 1 d = 86 400 s)뿐 — 영국 법 숫자 없음.
 * 급여 시간 반올림·근로시간 규칙은 gov.uk 원문을 열지 않아 쓰지 않는다. 본문 예시 숫자는 전부 frontmatter 에서 lib 함수로 계산한다.
 */
import BaseHead from "../../../../components/BaseHead.astro";
import AdSlot from "../../../../components/AdSlot.astro";
import ClarityHead from "../../../../components/ClarityHead.astro";
import GtagHead from "../../../../components/GtagHead.astro";
import TrackLinks from "../../../../components/TrackLinks.astro";
import AdSenseHead from "../../../../components/AdSenseHead.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import "../../../../styles/global.css";
import {
  toNanos,
  partsToNanos,
  convertAll,
  readableEn,
  withUnitEn,
  clock,
  minSec,
  UNITS,
  UNIT_NAME_EN,
} from "../../../../lib/sec-convert";

const title = "Seconds to Minutes Converter UK — Hours, Days, Milliseconds and Decimal Hours";
const description =
  "Convert seconds to minutes, minutes to hours and milliseconds to seconds in one go, with a UK timesheet decimal hours chart and Excel, SQL and Python formulas.";

const EX = "3725";
const n0 = toNanos(EX, "s")!;
const all0 = convertAll(n0);
const p0 = partsToNanos("", "1", "30", "")!;
const pAll0 = convertAll(p0);
const pMs0 = minSec(p0);

/** 본문 예시 — 450초, 7.6시간, 2일 3시간, 250,000밀리초 */
const S450 = toNanos("450", "s")!;
const S450all = convertAll(S450);
const H76 = toNanos("7.6", "h")!;
const D1 = convertAll(toNanos("1", "d")!);
const S200k = toNanos("200000", "s")!;
const S200kAll = convertAll(S200k);
const MS250 = toNanos("250000", "ms")!;
const MS250all = convertAll(MS250);
const MS1 = convertAll(toNanos("1", "ms")!);
const H30 = toNanos("30", "h")!;
const fmt = (m: string) => Number(m).toLocaleString("en-GB");
---

<html lang="en-GB">
  <head>
    <BaseHead title={title} description={description} />
    <ClarityHead />
    <GtagHead />
    <AdSenseHead />
  </head>
  <body class="bg-texture">
    <SiteHeader />
    <main>
      <h1>Seconds to minutes converter — and hours, days and milliseconds</h1>
      <p>
        Enter an amount and choose what it is measured in. The converter shows the same stretch of time in milliseconds, seconds,
        minutes, hours and days, spells it out as hours, minutes and seconds, and gives an h:mm:ss reading. Every figure works
        from 60 seconds to the minute, 3,600 to the hour and 86,400 to the day, and it recalculates as you type.
      </p>

      <div class="calc-card" data-two>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="val">Amount</label>
          <input type="text" id="val" inputmode="decimal" autocomplete="off" value={EX} placeholder="e.g. 3725" />
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="unit">Unit</label>
          <select id="unit" data-seg>
            {UNITS.map((u) => <option value={u} selected={u === "s"}>{UNIT_NAME_EN[u]}</option>)}
          </select>
        </div>

        <div class="result-hero" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true" data-dock="hero-value">
          <p class="label" id="hero-label">{withUnitEn(all0.s, "s")} is</p>
          <p class="big" id="hero-value">{readableEn(n0)}</p>
          <p class="hint" id="hero-sub">{withUnitEn(all0.min, "min")} · h:mm:ss {clock(n0)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="stat-row" data-receipt id="receipt">
          {UNITS.map((u) => (
            <div class="stat-chip">
              <span class="label">{UNIT_NAME_EN[u]}</span>
              <span class="value" id={`v-${u}`}>{all0[u]}</span>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="hint" id="err" role="alert"></p>

        <div class="actions">
          <button id="btn-download" type="button">Save as PNG</button>
          <button id="btn-share" type="button" class="secondary">Share</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 id="parts">Days, hours, minutes and seconds → one total</h2>
      <p>Got a time already split into parts, such as a lap time or a recording length? Fill in any of the boxes below.</p>
      <div class="calc-card" data-two>
        <div class="sc-parts">
          <div class="calc-input-wrap">
            <label for="p-d">Days</label>
            <input type="text" id="p-d" inputmode="decimal" autocomplete="off" placeholder="0" />
          </div>
          <div class="calc-input-wrap">
            <label for="p-h">Hours</label>
            <input type="text" id="p-h" inputmode="decimal" autocomplete="off" value="1" placeholder="0" />
          </div>
          <div class="calc-input-wrap">
            <label for="p-m">Minutes</label>
            <input type="text" id="p-m" inputmode="decimal" autocomplete="off" value="30" placeholder="0" />
          </div>
          <div class="calc-input-wrap">
            <label for="p-s">Seconds</label>
            <input type="text" id="p-s" inputmode="decimal" autocomplete="off" placeholder="0" />
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="result-hero" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">
          <p class="label">Total</p>
          <p class="big" id="p-value">{withUnitEn(pAll0.s, "s")}</p>
          <p class="hint" id="p-sub">
            {withUnitEn(pAll0.min, "min")}{pMs0.seconds === "0" ? "" : ` (${pMs0.minutes.toLocaleString("en-US")} min ${pMs0.seconds} sec)`} · {withUnitEn(pAll0.h, "h")} · {readableEn(p0)}
          </p>
        </div>
        <p class="hint" id="p-err" role="alert"></p>
      </div>

      <h2>The seconds to minutes formula</h2>
      <p>
        Minutes = seconds ÷ 60. A 450-second timer is therefore {S450all.min} minutes, which reads as {readableEn(S450)}. To go
        back, multiply: minutes × 60 = seconds.
      </p>
      <p>
        The trap is the decimal point. {S450all.min} minutes is <strong>not</strong> 7 minutes 50 seconds. The digits after the
        point are a share of a minute, so multiply them by 60 to get seconds: 0.5 × 60 = 30. In the same way 0.25 minutes is 15
        seconds and 0.1 minutes is 6 seconds.
      </p>

      <h2>Decimal hours chart for timesheets</h2>
      <p>
        Many timesheet and payroll tools ask for hours as a decimal rather than hours and minutes. Divide the minutes by 60 to
        get the decimal part. The chart covers the usual quarter-hours and the six-minute steps that make up tenths of an hour.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Minutes</th><th>Decimal hours</th><th>Hours and minutes</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {["6", "12", "15", "18", "24", "30", "36", "42", "45", "48", "54", "75", "450"].map((m) => {
            const n = toNanos(m, "min")!;
            return (
              <tr>
                <td>{fmt(m)}</td>
                <td>{convertAll(n).h}</td>
                <td>{readableEn(n)}</td>
              </tr>
            );
          })}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Decimal hours to hours and minutes works the other way round: keep the whole number as hours and multiply what is left
        by 60. A shift logged as 7.6 hours is 7 hours + 0.6 × 60 = {readableEn(H76)}. Pick "Hours" in the converter and type
        7.6 to check one quickly. Whether your employer rounds clock-in times, and to what, is set by your employer or payroll
        software, not by this converter.
      </p>

      <h2>Converting seconds to days, hours, minutes and seconds</h2>
      <p>Work from the largest unit down and pass the remainder along. Take 200,000 seconds:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>200,000 ÷ 86,400 = 2 days, remainder 27,200</li>
        <li>27,200 ÷ 3,600 = 7 hours, remainder 1,200</li>
        <li>1,200 ÷ 60 = 20 minutes, remainder 0</li>
        <li>So 200,000 seconds = <strong>{readableEn(S200k)}</strong>, or {withUnitEn(S200kAll.h, "h")}</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        One day is 24 hours, {D1.min} minutes or {D1.s} seconds. The h:mm:ss reading does not roll over into days, so 30 hours
        shows as {clock(H30)} — handy when you are adding up a week of working hours.
      </p>

      <h2>Milliseconds to seconds, minutes and hours</h2>
      <p>
        Milliseconds ÷ 1,000 = seconds; ÷ 60,000 = minutes; ÷ 3,600,000 = hours. A stopwatch or log entry of 250,000 ms is
        {" "}{MS250all.s} seconds, {MS250all.min} minutes, or {readableEn(MS250)}. Choose "Milliseconds" as the unit to convert one.
        Results are shown to six decimal places, so a single millisecond is {MS1.s} seconds and rounds to {MS1.min} minutes.
      </p>

      <h2>Excel and Google Sheets</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Seconds to minutes</strong>: <code>=A1/60</code> · <strong>seconds to hours</strong>: <code>=A1/3600</code></li>
        <li><strong>Minutes to hours</strong>: <code>=A1/60</code> · <strong>hours to minutes</strong>: <code>=A1*60</code></li>
        <li>
          <strong>Seconds as hh:mm:ss</strong>: <code>=A1/86400</code>, then apply the custom number format
          {" "}<code>[h]:mm:ss</code>. Spreadsheets store a time as a fraction of one day, hence the 86,400.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A time cell to decimal hours</strong>: if B1 holds 7:36, <code>=B1*24</code> gives 7.6 (format the result as a
          number, not a time).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Totals over 24 hours</strong>: the plain <code>h:mm:ss</code> format starts again at 0 after a day; the square
          brackets in <code>[h]</code> stop that. Google Sheets also offers Format → Number → Duration.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>In Python, JavaScript, SQL and bash</h2>
      <p>Each one is the same divide-and-remainder routine as above.</p>
      <pre><code>{`# Python
h, rem = divmod(seconds, 3600)
m, s = divmod(rem, 60)

// JavaScript
const h = Math.floor(seconds / 3600);
const m = Math.floor((seconds % 3600) / 60);
const s = seconds % 60;

-- PostgreSQL (returns an interval, e.g. 01:02:05)
SELECT 3725 * INTERVAL '1 second';

-- SQL Server (hh:mm:ss, wraps after 24 hours)
SELECT CONVERT(varchar(8), DATEADD(second, 3725, 0), 108);

# bash
printf '%d:%02d:%02d\\n' $((s/3600)) $((s%3600/60)) $((s%60))`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        If you start from milliseconds, divide by 1,000 first. Floating-point arithmetic can leave stray digits such as
        66.00000000000001 for 1.1 × 60; this converter counts in whole nanoseconds behind the scenes, so it does not.
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>Why does it stop at days — what about weeks, months and years?</summary>
        <p>
          A week is always 7 days, but months run from 28 to 31 days and years have 365 or 366, so they have no single number of
          seconds. For calendar time between two dates, use the <a href="/en-gb/tools/date-diff/">UK date calculator</a>.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Where do 60 and 3,600 seconds come from?</summary>
        <p>
          The International Bureau of Weights and Measures (BIPM) lists the minute, hour and day in Table 8 of its SI Brochure as
          non-SI units accepted for use with the SI: 1 min = 60 s, 1 h = 60 min = 3,600 s and 1 d = 24 h = 86,400 s.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Does the clock change in October or March make a day longer or shorter?</summary>
        <p>
          On the day the clocks go back or forward the time on your clock jumps by an hour, but a converted length of time does
          not change: 1 day is always treated here as 86,400 seconds. If you need the real elapsed time across a clock change,
          count it in UTC — the <a href="/en-gb/tools/unix-time/">Unix timestamp converter</a> works that way.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Can I type commas, such as 1,440.25?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes — commas and spaces are ignored and decimals are read to six places. Use a full stop for the decimal point. Negative
          numbers are rejected, as a length of time cannot be less than zero.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Will it convert minutes and seconds of an angle?</summary>
        <p>
          No. Degrees, minutes and seconds of latitude or longitude (1° = 60′ = 3,600″) borrow the same names but measure angles,
          not time.
        </p>
      </details>

      <p>
        Counting down to a date instead? Try the <a href="/en-gb/tools/dday/">days until countdown</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Source: <a href="https://www.bipm.org/en/publications/si-brochure" rel="noopener">BIPM, The International System of Units (SI
        Brochure), Table 8: Non-SI units accepted for use with the SI</a> (checked 20 October 2026)
      </p>

      <AdSlot label="sec-convert-result-en-gb" />
    </main>
    <SiteFooter />
    <style>
      .sc-parts { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 0 10px; }
      @media (max-width: 560px) { .sc-parts { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); } }
      pre { overflow-x: auto; }
    </style>
    <script src="../../../../scripts/sec-convert.ts"></script>
    <TrackLinks />
    <script src="../../../../scripts/calc-ui.ts"></script>
  </body>
</html>
